77 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523887 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186523535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523887 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c2e041ab-9982-589c-bc56-123e99e4c001" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523901 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523901 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523901 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186523715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523901 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "de7929f6-25fe-54ba-932a-cb75244104ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523904 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523904 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523904 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523904 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523904 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523904 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523904 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523904 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186523382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523904 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e1873516-1ea7-5025-ba34-62e81accf8f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523913 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523913 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523913 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186523424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523913 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f3e14d43-5876-53c9-8df1-5009e3c0d56b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523934 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523934 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523934 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523934 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/48/483c019e9c68f857379192d2b40ab84803c4360ca4e0158adc0a8a3bc1636c4e.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}xy, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523934 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c8173cc-49c9-5f6d-b758-9380f0f1613b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523934 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/ff/ffba85fc6ae71a1a5c250a90d686e8852c77756eebd228f3d3a2a9622cba01e1.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<\\frac{1}{2},Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nHowever, $f(u,v)$ is zero everywhere inside the region $Δ.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523941 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523941 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523941 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523941 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(1+xy), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $y>2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523941 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27951774-1d49-5cba-9914-8523d1f1e8cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523941 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $y>2$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/f5/f5ceaa24d74de28cc24de05ab352fbe4586c4c83630d6163f4871ccbe74e2cd2.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{3}(1+uv)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[u+\\frac{{u}^{2}v}{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+\\frac{{x}^{2}v}{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[xv+\\frac{{x}^{2}{v}^{2}}{4}]}_{v=0}^{v=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x+{x}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523944 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523944 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143707" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523944 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523944 #edb/ref 1071 "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(5,4)=1,\\,F(5,1)=\\frac{2}{9},\\,F(4,4)=\\frac{1}{3},\\,F(4,1)=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(4\\le X\\le 5,1\\le Y\\le 4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523944 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ebdf296-e322-505e-9c4f-5149cacb6a7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523944 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/13/137e3d2e3344f5d1f57f36d470564a518c359bb46d5278382b7242e73999e80e.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,4).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(5,1)$ and $(4,4).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4\\le X\\le 5,1\\le Y\\le 4) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(5,4)-F(5,1)-F(4,4)+F(4,1) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{3}{9}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523947 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523947 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143703" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523947 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523947 #edb/ref 1071 "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(1,5)=0.63,\\,F(0,5)=0.5,\\,F(-2,5)=0.27.\n$$\n\nFind $P(-2\\le X\\le 1,Y\\le 5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523947 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "419b7449-2db5-5f6c-9da4-4ea57433c3f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523947 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/03/0378964a9f167554e4faeba228ebae485de94dda83fbd6704a864a06b8abe77a.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,5)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(-2,5)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/90/9079baa6464df773cacb820a6bb4a8e2b8f4ecc19755619a9b5c191a0c48507f.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2\\le X\\le 1,Y\\le 5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,5)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 5)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(-2,5)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le -2,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & =F(1,5)-F(-2,5) \\\\  & =0.63-0.27 \\\\  & =0.36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523951 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523951 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523951 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523951 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/eb/eb5f10ce887ba9d167b2753618064fcdbf8d1f42371d733aacd56a8d8cb5e667.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523951 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4bec72aa-1d05-5e53-b2ca-0df4a3dfbe51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523951 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/a5/a5f876c9351040845fcc5e6839f4ca776fe22988a5e4c814d4a7b063692f083c.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(0,\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<0,Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(\\frac{9}{2}{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{[\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{3}{v}^{2}]}_{u=-1}^{u=0}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(0+\\frac{3}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{v}^{3}}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523960 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523960 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523960 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523960 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}, & 1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>3$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523960 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "61f58f19-0648-54c0-b55e-2caca7ea1507" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523960 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/1f/1fbbe9e4728cd5aefcc456a6c8666e3e2e6c0d0bc832f471677d908cf6c012e3.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le y\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{1}^{y}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{y}v{[-\\frac{1}{u}]}_{u=1}^{u=3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{y}v(1-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{y}\\frac{2}{3}v\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{v=1}^{v=y} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523975 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523975 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143657" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523975 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523975 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & 1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<0,\\,y>\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523975 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ed10907-9632-53b1-9eec-d2c7cfd4e80a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523975 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<0,\\,y>\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/4f/4f5cfe5e819f9479199ba1af6116627b31d03c88d8aa4c6e049bb375316c2c10.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=∅.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo there is nowhere in our region $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{Δ}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524003 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524003 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143693" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524003 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524003 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}({y}^{2}+xy), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524003 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1b6ab32-2ee9-5286-82fb-e9633b19fc28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524003 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/fc/fcfa6842026fc1635efe939525d60a9c83c89735a1a1e69d32d1b31c8431f02c.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le y\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{5}({v}^{2}+uv)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{5}({v}^{2}+uv)\\,\\text{d}u\\,]\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}{[\\frac{3}{5}({v}^{2}u+\\frac{{u}^{2}v}{2})]}_{u=0}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{3}{5}(x{v}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}v}{2})\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{[\\frac{x{v}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{v}^{2}}{4}]}_{v=0}^{v=y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524005 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524005 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143656" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524005 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524005 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>1,\\,y<0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524005 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7fd97ca-9b98-50c0-b683-53f1d4a2aa1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524005 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>1,\\,y<0,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/12/1232f75c56b548896fc3c0371e36201eb24c71811d59d185440a964e393818be.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=∅.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo there is nowhere in our region $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{Δ}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524007 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524007 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143742" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524007 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524007 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/eb/eb5f10ce887ba9d167b2753618064fcdbf8d1f42371d733aacd56a8d8cb5e667.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524007 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c246e56e-54f4-5ef4-b7e3-0ed423a3b4da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524007 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/53/53d0466b98c4b9b2704cc2f7610e7f28cee6ec27aaf2ea94be75c5b3f32a8257.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(1,\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<1,Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\frac{9}{2}{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{[\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{3}{v}^{2}]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}[\\frac{3}{2}{v}^{2}-(-\\frac{3}{2}{v}^{2})]\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}3{v}^{2}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[{v}^{3}]}_{v=0}^{v=1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524016 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524016 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143740" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524016 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524016 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/eb/eb5f10ce887ba9d167b2753618064fcdbf8d1f42371d733aacd56a8d8cb5e667.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524016 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d405caca-881b-5a05-9419-094e813c3d9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524016 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/dd/dd7024bcd41ce8312f484763e0988ebcceb54e7154cd8ee3c209991bce5727c1.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<-\\frac{3}{2},Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nHowever, $f(u,v)$ is zero everywhere inside the region $Δ.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524024 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524024 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143716" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524024 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524024 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2}+y, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $y>1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524024 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e52f8c43-5915-56b1-841a-9a33ac8f7470" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524024 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $y>1$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/12/12e26559137d7c494c5ddc6f1add55f646dd84236f355221042448ddeacd2879.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{x}u{v}^{2}+v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}{2}+uv]}_{u=1}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{{x}^{2}{v}^{2}}{2}+xv-\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}-v]\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{{v}^{2}({x}^{2}-1)}{2}+v(x-1)]\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{v}^{3}({x}^{2}-1)}{6}+\\frac{{v}^{2}(x-1)}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{6}+\\frac{x-1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524025 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524025 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524025 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524025 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{12}x{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524025 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e58401f9-a4c1-5465-9216-a1679c479a59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524025 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/e4/e4aef49bebaf250fa93e0ad1daccbad324bf714c7e5627f5245ea8387bc8da87.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le y\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{12}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{1}{12}{v}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}u\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{1}{12}{v}^{2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{2}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{{v}^{3}}{3}]}_{v=0}^{v=y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524026 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524026 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143701" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524026 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524026 #edb/ref 1071 "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(8,10)=\\frac{9}{10},\\,F(8,8)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,F(8,4)=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 8,4\\le Y\\le 8).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524026 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e662cd14-0105-5064-96eb-7a2f86c8b78d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524026 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/d3/d31cdb5e3f201d9dd5b3b622d883f67bb2c8bc0a5036905695a5cc5ceb3bda07.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,8)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/62/626d5c4cba7b41d6b6f2da77ca1d62c5bcd442990ce31cf630e69882dd6580b8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 8,4\\le Y\\le 8) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,8)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 8)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(8,8)-F(8,4) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524033 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524033 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143658" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524033 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524033 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{e}^{y}, & -1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>2,\\,y>\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524033 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f06b7511-18eb-5f50-9aea-3636371eb6fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524033 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>2,\\,y>\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/19/19510c5eb927edbc93ff077f13e645820548e9074da0e1101d9ef641650642bb.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=D.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo our region $Δ$ covers the entire region where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{D}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
